// Title: an abstract constructor pattern takes its type parameters from its payload, so it never matches
// Command: timeout -s KILL 30 /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-10-19/bin/graphix --no-cache x-typecheck-patterns-abstract-payload-params.gx
// Expected: [0, 1, 1, 2] -- Box(null) is 0, Box(5) is 1; Box(`A) is 1, Box(`B) is 2.
// Observed: g is refused: "unreachable arm: the earlier arms already cover the
//   whole scrutinee" at Box(`B). With g removed, f prints [1, 1]: the arm
//   Box(null) never matches Box(null). And `|x: Box<[`A, `B]>| select x
//   { Box(`A) => 1 }` is accepted and matches neither Box(`A) nor Box(`B)
//   ("no arm matches ..: a coverage hole in the check").
// Cause: the arm's predicate is Box<null> / Box<`A> (params from the payload
//   pattern), and the run-time test (Type::is_a, Abstract arm) compares
//   parameters invariantly with the value's [i64, null] / [`A, `B]. The same
//   root with an irrefutable payload over two instantiations:
//   design/review-2026-10-19/repro/x-typecheck-patterns-abstract-bind-two-insts.gx
type Box<'a> = Abstract<'a>;
let f = |x: Box<[i64, null]>| select x { Box(null) => 0, Box(n) => 1 };
let g = |x: Box<[`A, `B]>| select x { Box(`A) => 1, Box(`B) => 2 };
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:100.ms, 0));
[f(Box(null)), f(Box(5)), g(Box(`A)), g(Box(`B))]
